# Model and mode router

Re-evaluate on every user message, including follow-ups. You cannot change the
model picker or Cursor mode; the user must.

| Axis | Light | Mid | Heavy |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Model | Single-file fixes, config/docs, simple CRUD, rename, Q&A | Multi-file work, non-trivial debug, unfamiliar code, API/integration | Architecture, high-risk production, deep unclear bugs, security-critical |
| Mode | Read-only Q&A → Ask | Implementation or multi-step edits → Agent | Open design trade-offs → Plan |

Default to the lightest capable model. Never pick Heavy because it is available.
Never stay Light when correctness is at risk.

If the current model is heavier or lighter than recommended, or the current
mode cannot complete the task:

1. Do not call tools, edit files, or start implementation.
2. State current vs recommended model tier and mode, and one-line why.
3. Ask the user to switch, stay, or change mode.
4. Wait for an explicit preference.

If current model and mode already match, execute immediately.

After confirmation, execute with the user's choice. If they stay on a mismatch,
note the risk in one sentence, then proceed. If they will switch, stop until
the next message on the new model or mode.

Never sacrifice correctness for cost.
